00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.202
One major shortcoming of ColPali

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:04.871
is that it requires significantly more memory than other techniques

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:08.375
in order to store all the vectors for each document.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:11.177
As a result, optimization techniques

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.780
are used to reduce ColPali's memory footprint.

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:16.983
Let's see what those approaches look like.

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:19.986
The first is Scalar or Binary Quantization,

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.855
which converts the floating point numbers

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:23.990
in each vector to a condensed format,

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.993
like a 4-bit integer or even 1 bit.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:29.696
The second approach is Row or Column Pooling.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:33.400
Patches are grouped together by rows or columns,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.003
and the vectors in each group are pooled, usually by averaging,

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:38.705
to create a single vector.

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:41.341
The final approach is Hierarchical Pooling,

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:44.644
which is essentially more intelligent Row/Column Pooling.

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:48.548
Patches are grouped with other patches that have similar embedding vectors.

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.651
You will start by loading the document embeddings.

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:53.953
In the previous lesson, you've learned

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.022
how ColPali converts PDF pages to screenshots

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:58.124
and generates multi-vector embeddings.

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.827
Here, you will use a helper function

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:03.029
that handles all of these steps internally.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.833
PDF conversion, image loading, and embedding generation.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.070
Since these details were covered in the previous lesson,

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.405
you can focus on what matters for this one,

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.973
optimization techniques.

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:18.078
You will use pre-computed embeddings to keep this lesson moving smoothly.

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:21.114
But you can also set LOAD_PRECOMPUTED to false

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:23.116
to see the entire process.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:25.585
You will load the ColPali processor

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:28.188
to demonstrate how ColPali structures

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:30.590
document images as patch grids.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.293
This will help you understand the spatial pooling techniques

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:35.095
we'll explore later on.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:38.198
Another helper function handles all the complexity,

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:42.302
either loading precomputed embeddings from Parquet files,

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.405
or generating them fresh by converting PDFs,

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:49.008
loading the ColPali model and processing images in batches.

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:52.145
load_or_compute_image_embeddings function

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:54.347
returns a data frame with image paths

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:57.150
and embeddings already converted to NumPy.

00:01:57.851 --> 00:02:00.854
Here is how some of the entries look like.

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:03.823
Obviously, since you are working with images,

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:06.526
we can also display them on the screen.

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:12.866
This slides are coming from different Deeplearning AI courses.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:15.535
So if you have ever took any of them,

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:17.937
after completing this one, you will be ready

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:20.240
to just ask question directly to the slides.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:24.577
Each page generates around thousand token embeddings.

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:28.047
That's a lot of vectors to store and search over,

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:31.951
but let's see how the individual shapes of the embeddings look like.

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:36.289
It's consistently 1031 vectors per image.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:39.492
You can now implement different optimization techniques,

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:43.897
one by one, and eventually compare them on the same data set.

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:47.100
Let's start with scalar and binary quantization.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.503
When we calculate the amount of memory needed to store the vectors,

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:54.274
a common factor is the size of each individual dimension.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:58.311
Standard embeddings use 4-byte float32 values per dimension,

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:02.615
but if you could reduce that size without sacrificing search quality,

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:07.020
you would have an easy way to save significant RAM.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.522
Scalar and binary quantization techniques

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:12.392
aim to represent vectors using either one byte

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:15.395
integers or even just one bit per dimension,

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:19.899
and they are widely used for regular dense embeddings from bi-encoders.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:24.571
Scalar quantization compresses float32 values, which are 4 bytes,

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:27.674
down to int8 values, which are just 1 byte,

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:29.876
achieving four times compression.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:34.647
Scalar quantization works by mapping the continuous float range

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.849
to discrete integer buckets.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.252
For each dimension, the quantizer learns

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:42.155
the minimum and maximum values across the entire data set

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.257
and then linearly maps this range

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:47.560
to the 0 to 255 integer space.

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:51.464
For example, if dimension 5 ranges from

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:56.169
negative 0.8 to 1.2 across all vectors,

00:03:56.269 --> 00:04:01.574
a value of 0.2 would map to approximately 128.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:04.978
This makes scalar quantization dataset aware.

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:07.780
It requires analyzing all vectors,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.284
or at least a representative sample, to determine the optimal ranges.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:15.788
Fortunately, vector search engines like Qdrant handle this process internally.

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:19.792
We simply configure a quantization at the collection level,

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.794
send our original float embeddings,

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:25.298
and the engine handles the compression and transformation automatically.

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:28.735
Binary quantization takes compression even further,

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:32.305
reducing each float32 dimension, which is 4 bytes,

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:34.307
down to just one bit,

00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:37.310
achieving a dramatic 32 times compression ratio.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.513
The process is simple. Positive values become one,

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:43.516
while negative or zero values become zero.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:47.253
This extreme compression transforms similarity calculations

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:49.922
into efficient bitwise operations.

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.825
Binary quantization works particularly well

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:55.028
when embeddings are centered around zero

00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:57.430
with relatively symmetric distributions,

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.833
which is common for normalized neural network outputs.

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:03.369
However, this aggressive compression

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:05.471
does come with trade-offs.

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:07.774
Like scalar quantization,

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:10.343
Qdrant handles binary quantization internally.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.945
We configure it at the collection level

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:15.448
and set original float embeddings,

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:17.850
letting the engine perform the binary conversion.

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:22.088
A pooling approach can take advantage of ColPali's spatial structure.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:25.992
Remember from the previous lesson that ColPali processes document images

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:28.795
as a 32 by 32 grid of patches.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:33.132
Row pooling averages embeddings along each row of this grid,

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.835
while column pooling averages along each column.

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.438
This preserves spatial relationships

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:40.840
while dramatically reducing the number of vectors

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.542
to just 32.

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:45.478
Let's extract the image patch embeddings.

00:05:45.878 --> 00:05:49.882
The processor creates a sequence of a 1024 image patch tokens

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:52.485
from a 32 by 32 grid,

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:55.388
plus a few special tokens for the model's architecture.

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:59.592
For the pooling methods, we don't need this additional tokens.

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:02.729
So we can use the image mask from the processor

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:06.199
to extract only the image related token positions.

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:11.371
By filtering to just the image patch tokens using this mask,

00:06:11.471 --> 00:06:15.375
we can reshape the flat sequence of a 1024 embeddings

00:06:15.475 --> 00:06:19.779
into a 32 by 32 by 128 grid.

00:06:20.113 --> 00:06:23.282
Let's create a helper function that does exactly this.

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:26.285
Each position in this 32 by 32 grid

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:29.088
corresponds to a patch location in the original image,

00:06:29.489 --> 00:06:32.692
and each patch has a 128 dimensional embedding.

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:35.328
Let's test your reshaping function

00:06:35.328 --> 00:06:37.330
on the first document's embedding.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.133
You've just applied the embeddings_grid function

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:43.336
to verify it correctly transforms the mask embeddings.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:47.173
into the expected 32 by 32

00:06:47.173 --> 00:06:50.476
by 128 dimensional structure.

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:53.479
Once our embeddings are in a grid form,

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:55.782
you can implement row and column pooling

00:06:55.782 --> 00:06:57.784
as simple NumPy operations.

00:06:57.917 --> 00:07:01.487
Row pooling averages each of the 32 rows of the grid,

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.990
resulting in 32 representative vectors

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.692
that capture horizontal patterns across the document.

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:10.696
And similarly, column pooling averages each of the 32 columns,

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:15.401
capturing vertical patterns. Both methods reduce the total number of vectors

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:18.104
from 1024 to just 32.

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.506
It's not that easy to tell

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:22.575
anything just by looking at these numbers.

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:24.777
But just to confirm the shape is correct,

00:07:25.178 --> 00:07:28.481
you will run row_mean_pooling on the created grid,

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:31.984
and it's truly 32 by 128 dimensions.

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:35.421
The same goes for column_mean_pooling.

00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:37.523
After calling the method, we can see

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:39.625
that the shape matches the expected size.

00:07:39.625 --> 00:07:41.961
Nevertheless, please do not spend much time

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:44.263
looking at the numbers we can represent.

00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:46.866
Hierarchical token pooling uses clustering

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:49.268
to intelligently group similar embeddings.

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:53.473
The algorithm performs hierarchical clustering on the token embeddings,

00:07:53.573 --> 00:07:55.775
then averages each cluster

00:07:55.775 --> 00:07:57.977
into a single representative vector.

00:07:58.044 --> 00:08:00.546
Clusters are created from similar patches.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:03.549
So if you have a lot of background patches

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:05.852
of the same color, they should be grouped together.

00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:10.056
The ColPali engine library has an implementation of that technique available.

00:08:10.356 --> 00:08:13.059
Let me show you a simple example of how it works.

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:16.562
Assume you run Hierarchical Token Pooling on the image

00:08:17.063 --> 00:08:20.066
of nine patches. Each patch gets its own embedding,

00:08:20.166 --> 00:08:23.469
so our multivector representation is a sequence of nine vectors.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:26.072
If the pool factor is set to two,

00:08:26.272 --> 00:08:28.341
you create four clusters

00:08:28.641 --> 00:08:32.712
as 9 divided by 2 is 4.5, which rounds down to four.

00:08:33.112 --> 00:08:35.548
I'm making this up, but probably both patches

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:38.117
containing ears would end up in the same cluster.

00:08:38.518 --> 00:08:40.753
Similarly, the patches with just fur

00:08:41.053 --> 00:08:42.855
are also quite similar to each other,

00:08:43.055 --> 00:08:45.825
so it's quite likely they would also be grouped together.

00:08:46.092 --> 00:08:48.294
A tile with eyes is quite unique,

00:08:48.294 --> 00:08:50.863
so it's likely to be a single patch cluster.

00:08:51.264 --> 00:08:53.065
And so on, and so forth.

00:08:53.165 --> 00:08:56.269
Patches don't have to be contiguous to form a cluster.

00:08:56.869 --> 00:08:59.872
Actually, the pooling technique gets a sequence of embeddings

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:02.475
with no information about spatial relationships,

00:09:02.475 --> 00:09:04.877
so there is not even a way to do it.

00:09:05.278 --> 00:09:07.780
The ColPali engine library that you use

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.449
has an implementation of this technique available.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.753
So you will just create a helper function

00:09:13.753 --> 00:09:15.755
called hierarchical_token_pooling

00:09:15.755 --> 00:09:19.058
to have a simpler way of using it on our original embeddings.

00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:21.761
Let's run it on one of the examples

00:09:21.761 --> 00:09:23.763
that you have in the data set

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:25.798
to see how much memory we're

00:09:25.798 --> 00:09:28.167
able to save by using this method.

00:09:28.568 --> 00:09:31.070
With a pool factor of two, you've reduced the

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:35.074
1031 vectors down to 515.

00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:37.476
Cutting memory usage nearly in half

00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:40.780
while preserving the semantic information through clustering.

00:09:41.147 --> 00:09:43.249
Now comes the crucial experiment,

00:09:43.249 --> 00:09:45.952
comparing all our optimization strategies

00:09:45.952 --> 00:09:47.954
in a real vector database.

00:09:48.354 --> 00:09:51.757
We'll create a single Qdrant collection with multiple named vectors,

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:54.760
each representing a different optimization approach.

00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:57.964
This allows you to directly compare retrieval quality

00:09:57.964 --> 00:10:00.666
and memory usage across all strategies.

00:10:00.967 --> 00:10:03.769
Original embeddings, binary quantization,

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:07.974
hierarchical pooling at different factors, and spatial pooling.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:10.109
You will answer the key question,

00:10:10.109 --> 00:10:12.111
how much memory can we save

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:14.914
without sacrificing retrieval quality?

00:10:14.914 --> 00:10:18.317
First of all, let's make sure the collection does not exist.

00:10:18.684 --> 00:10:22.188
You will set up multiple named vectors in a single collection.

00:10:22.788 --> 00:10:25.191
Each vector will use multivector configuration

00:10:25.491 --> 00:10:27.693
with max sim comparison,

00:10:27.893 --> 00:10:30.029
but the number of vectors for

00:10:30.029 --> 00:10:32.498
pooling will vary depending on the strategy.

00:10:32.898 --> 00:10:37.169
For quantization methods, we'll enable them on the corresponding named vector.

00:10:37.169 --> 00:10:39.972
to compare against the original.

00:10:40.673 --> 00:10:43.876
Now, let's insert all our embeddings into the collection.

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:48.381
Each document will have seven different vector representations,

00:10:48.381 --> 00:10:52.685
allowing us to compare the retrieval performance side by side.

00:10:52.685 --> 00:10:56.088
The helper method loads precomputed vector data

00:10:56.088 --> 00:10:58.924
and also limits which slide decks are used

00:10:58.924 --> 00:11:01.127
to a subset of the entire list.

00:11:01.127 --> 00:11:04.263
To avoid loading all the vectors into memory,

00:11:04.263 --> 00:11:09.402
the helper function yields the examples one by one.

00:11:09.402 --> 00:11:12.471
You will run the same queries across all the optimization techniques

00:11:12.471 --> 00:11:15.174
you implemented to compare retrieval quality.

00:11:15.875 --> 00:11:18.277
The helper function handles all the complexity.

00:11:18.544 --> 00:11:22.248
It will load precomputed query embeddings or compute them fresh

00:11:22.248 --> 00:11:24.650
depending on the load precomputed flag.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.753
The queries, the model, and the processor

00:11:27.753 --> 00:11:31.057
are just created internally in the helper function.

00:11:31.824 --> 00:11:34.593
Now let's search with each vector configuration

00:11:34.593 --> 00:11:36.495
and compare the results side by side.

00:11:36.696 --> 00:11:38.898
You will retrieve the top three documents

00:11:38.898 --> 00:11:41.200
for each query and optimization strategy.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:44.303
So, let's define the order of the optimizations

00:11:44.303 --> 00:11:46.205
so we can refer to them easily.

00:11:46.706 --> 00:11:49.108
You won't apply token pooling on queries,

00:11:49.108 --> 00:11:52.511
as they are already limited in terms of the sequence length.

00:11:52.712 --> 00:11:55.514
Row and column pooling for queries do not make sense

00:11:55.514 --> 00:11:58.217
as there are no spatial relationships in text.

00:11:58.451 --> 00:12:01.554
Moreover, Qdrant handles quantization internally.

00:12:01.954 --> 00:12:05.357
So in all the cases, the call to Qdrant API

00:12:05.424 --> 00:12:07.827
uses the query embeddings directly.

00:12:08.127 --> 00:12:11.330
Thus, we have a helper method that runs the same query

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:14.433
on all the vectors and presents them as a matrix.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:19.004
Let's visualize the results with actual document images side by side.

00:12:19.705 --> 00:12:23.008
This comparison shows precision metrics with color coding

00:12:23.008 --> 00:12:27.113
to quickly identify which optimizations might maintain retrieval quality.

00:12:28.914 --> 00:12:32.218
Precision here measures what percentage of the retrieved documents

00:12:32.218 --> 00:12:34.520
match those returned by the baseline,

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:36.522
which is original embeddings.

00:12:37.022 --> 00:12:40.993
That shows how well each optimization preserves retrieval accuracy.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:44.063
Our first query, coffee mug, returns

00:12:44.063 --> 00:12:46.866
this set of documents as the top three matches.

00:12:47.266 --> 00:12:49.268
Scalar quantization, binary quantization,

00:12:49.268 --> 00:12:51.470
and hierarchical token pooling were all able

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.774
to return the same set of results.

00:12:54.974 --> 00:12:57.276
Sometimes the order was slightly different.

00:12:57.276 --> 00:13:01.580
Still, the precision does not measure the order of the documents.

00:13:01.881 --> 00:13:05.684
It focuses only on the fact of being relevant or not.

00:13:05.985 --> 00:13:08.587
However, for column_pooled and row_pooled,

00:13:08.587 --> 00:13:11.190
we are getting slightly different set of results,

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:14.193
and we can clearly say that at least column_pooled

00:13:14.193 --> 00:13:15.995
didn't do this job really well.

00:13:16.328 --> 00:13:17.997
Since you only have three queries,

00:13:17.997 --> 00:13:19.932
you can run them one by one.

00:13:20.833 --> 00:13:22.635
It's time for the second one.

00:13:23.135 --> 00:13:25.137
Again, scalar quantization

00:13:25.337 --> 00:13:27.840
was able to return the same set of documents,

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.442
but all the other methods struggled with that.

00:13:30.943 --> 00:13:33.546
Especially column pooling was not even able to pick

00:13:33.646 --> 00:13:36.248
one of the documents that were marked as relevant

00:13:36.515 --> 00:13:38.517
by the original ColPali embeddings.

00:13:39.051 --> 00:13:41.554
Last but not least, let's check the last query,

00:13:42.555 --> 00:13:44.857
which is one learning algorithm.

00:13:45.858 --> 00:13:49.862
Scalar quantization still provides exactly the same set of results

00:13:49.862 --> 00:13:52.364
like the original ColPali embeddings.

00:13:52.865 --> 00:13:56.068
Similarly, hierarchical token pooling was able to

00:13:56.068 --> 00:13:58.871
return the same set of results

00:13:58.871 --> 00:14:00.873
like the original baseline vectors.

00:14:01.273 --> 00:14:04.043
Unfortunately, row and column pooling both

00:14:04.043 --> 00:14:06.545
do not seem to be working that fine,

00:14:06.545 --> 00:14:08.547
at least not for our data set.

00:14:08.814 --> 00:14:12.318
However in practice, we don't look at the quality of retrieval

00:14:12.318 --> 00:14:15.020
for specific cases, but we calculate the metrics

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:17.990
for all the test examples globally.

00:14:18.591 --> 00:14:20.459
The average precision at five for

00:14:20.459 --> 00:14:22.595
all the tested methods look like this.

00:14:22.995 --> 00:14:25.297
Column and Row Pooling didn't perform well

00:14:25.297 --> 00:14:28.601
as they were rarely able to select the most relevant document

00:14:28.601 --> 00:14:31.604
as you saw in the examples in our notebook.

00:14:31.804 --> 00:14:35.007
Hierarchical Token Pooling works significantly better,

00:14:35.007 --> 00:14:36.542
and the pool factor does not

00:14:36.542 --> 00:14:38.310
even seem to impact it too much.

00:14:38.611 --> 00:14:41.013
So we could possibly increase it even more.

00:14:41.614 --> 00:14:44.316
Surprisingly, a simple Scalar Quantization method

00:14:44.316 --> 00:14:48.320
was able to consistently return the same documents as the baseline,

00:14:48.754 --> 00:14:52.625
making it a promising approach that does not require any preprocessing

00:14:53.325 --> 00:14:55.828
as it can be configured at a collection level only.

00:14:56.328 --> 00:14:58.964
Binary Quantization wasn't the greatest method out there,

00:14:59.331 --> 00:15:02.334
but given its huge possible impact on the memory usage

00:15:02.434 --> 00:15:05.537
and processing speed, it might be considered in some cases.

00:15:06.038 --> 00:15:09.241
It's worth mentioning that you can combine multiple memory optimizations

00:15:09.241 --> 00:15:10.843
of a different kind.

00:15:11.043 --> 00:15:14.847
Scalar Quantization might be also enabled for the embeddings

00:15:14.847 --> 00:15:16.949
after applying Hierarchical Token Pooling

00:15:17.049 --> 00:15:19.852
if you see the quality is still acceptable.

00:15:20.119 --> 00:15:24.723
Nevertheless, a real benchmark should not focus on three handpicked examples

00:15:24.924 --> 00:15:28.027
but be broader. Still, evaluation is key

00:15:28.127 --> 00:15:30.729
and dependent on the datasets you are working on.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:33.232
Experiment with different techniques to see

00:15:33.232 --> 00:15:35.935
which combinations work best in your data.
